<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#penal-transportation">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Penal transportation</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Penal transportation was the punishment of sending convicted criminals to a distant colony. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> transported about 50,000 convicts to its American colonies before 1776 and about 162,000 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> between 1788 and 1868, mainly to New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-wales">South Wales</a>, Van Diemen's Land and later Western Australia. Convict labour built much of early colonial Australia until opposition from free settlers brought the system to an end.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/penal-transportation.jpg" alt="Penal transportation" width="320" height="266" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sayer, Robert, 1725–1794 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black-eyed_Sue_and_Sweet_Poll_of_Plymouth_taking_leave_of_their_lovers_who_are_going_to_Botany_Bay.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Banishment had been a punishment since at least the 5th century BCE in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> transportation arose under the Bloody Code of the 17th to early 19th centuries, when many offences carried death and judges had few other sentences. Offenders thought too minor to hang were pardoned, and transportation was legally a condition of the pardon rather than a sentence in itself, presented as a public show of the king's mercy. In 1615, under James I, a council committee gained the power to choose prisoners fit for service beyond the seas, and by 1670 transportation could be imposed for life or for a set term.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>From the early 1600s until 1776 convicts went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies">West Indies</a> and the mainland colonies, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a> receiving more than any other province. In the 17th century the convicts or the shipowners bore the cost of the voyage. The Transportation Act 1717 allowed seven-year sentences to America, made returning early a capital offence, and from 1720 the Crown paid merchants to carry the convicts. Prisoners of war from Ireland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> were sold into indentured servitude.</p><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a> ended this, the prisons filled and old ships moored in ports served as hulks. After trials at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-coast-castle">Cape Coast Castle</a> and Gorée in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> the government turned to New South Wales, and the First Fleet sailed in 1787. A convict there who had served part of a term could apply for a ticket of leave, which allowed some to marry and raise a family. Few could afford to return home once released.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>As the policy grew unpopular in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a> was considered instead, but the Convict Crisis of 1849 kept southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> from becoming a penal colony. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> sent convicts to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devils-island">Devil's Island</a> in Guiana from 1852 to 1953 and about 22,000 criminals and political prisoners, many of them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communards">Communards</a>, to New Caledonia until 1897; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-dreyfus">Alfred Dreyfus</a> was held on Devil's Island. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> deported up to 1.9 million people from its western republics during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga-germans">Volga Germans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chechens">Chechens</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Penal transportation” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Transportation</b> ended in 1840, and self-government came in 1856.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-new-south-wales">Colony of New South Wales</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Transportation</b> ended in 1853, and in 1856 the colony won self-government and adopted the name Tasmania.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-tasmania">Colony of Tasmania</a></li></ul></section>
